        "service_ip2": {
            "type": "subnet_attr",
            "subnet": "_service",
            "attr": "host",
            "index": 2
        },
        "vc_id": {
            "type": "choice",
            "options": "vc_id_options"
        }
    },
    "scenario": "MPLS L2VPN EoMPLS Port Mode Pseudowire",
    "goals": "• {{router2_name}}, {{router3_name}} and {{router4_name}} form an MPLS backbone where OSPF area 0, the Loopback0 addresses and LDP are already configured and operational on the two core links {{link2_cidr}} and {{link3_cidr}}.\n    provider: {{router2_name}} (PE), {{router3_name}} (P), {{router4_name}} (PE)\n    customer: {{router1_name}} and {{router5_name}}, both inside the single subnet {{service_cidr}} ({{service_ip1}} and {{service_ip2}})\n• {{router1_name}} and {{router5_name}} are not routers of the provider network and run no routing protocol at all: they expect to be in the same layer 2 segment, and they are two hops apart in the physical topology.\n• The customer-facing interfaces of the two PEs carry no IP address and are already up.\n• Deliver the layer 2 service as an Ethernet over MPLS pseudowire in port mode: bind the whole customer-facing interface of each PE to a pseudowire towards the remote PE Loopback0, using MPLS encapsulation and virtual circuit identifier {{vc_id}} on both ends.\n    {{router2_name}} peers with {{router4_loopback0_ip}}\n    {{router4_name}} peers with {{router2_loopback0_ip}}\n• Do not give the customer-facing PE interfaces an IP address and do not route the customer subnet: {{router1_name}} and {{router5_name}} must reach each other as direct layer 2 neighbours.\n• The virtual circuit must come up on both PEs and {{router1_name}} must be able to ping {{service_ip2}}.",
    "topology_model": {
